Total gastrectomy is a common surgical procedure for patients diagnosed with gastric cancer. This procedure involves the complete removal of the stomach, which is often necessary for treating advanced cases of gastric cancer. The relationship between total gastrectomy and gastric cancer is well-established, as it is a standard intervention aimed at improving survival rates. The implications of this surgery are profound, as it not only affects the patient's physical health but also their nutritional status and overall quality of life post-surgery. [Data: Entities (13420); Relationships (16620)]

