This community encompasses various entities related to food intolerances and allergies, highlighting their interconnections and the implications for health and nutrition. Key entities include food intolerances, allergies, the immune system, and scientific societies focused on these issues, indicating a complex network of relationships that influence public health.

## Food Intolerances and Allergies are distinct yet related conditions

Food intolerances and allergies represent two different types of adverse reactions to food. Intolerances, such as lactose intolerance, do not involve the immune system and are often due to enzyme deficiencies, while allergies involve an immune response to specific food proteins. Understanding this distinction is crucial for effective diagnosis and management. The relationship between these two conditions is significant, as they can co-occur and influence each other, complicating treatment and dietary management strategies. [Data: Entities (14001, 9026); Relationships (17410, 17417)]

## The role of the immune system in allergies

The immune system plays a critical role in the development and manifestation of allergies. Allergic reactions are mediated by the immune system, which can be influenced by genetic factors and environmental conditions. The development of the immune system in children is particularly important, as it can affect their susceptibility to food allergies. Additionally, changes in the intestinal microbiota due to antibiotic use can impact immune function, further influencing the likelihood of developing allergies. [Data: Entities (9027, 9028); Relationships (14494, 17409)]

## Children are particularly vulnerable to food allergies

Children are the most affected demographic when it comes to food allergies, indicating a direct relationship between age and the prevalence of these conditions. This vulnerability necessitates careful monitoring and management of dietary exposures in children to prevent allergic reactions. The implications of food allergies in children can be severe, affecting their quality of life and requiring ongoing medical attention. [Data: Entities (9026); Relationships (9988)]

