The community focuses on the anatomical structures of the pharynx and esophagus, which are critical in the swallowing process. The pharynx serves as a conduit for food to the esophagus, highlighting the interconnectedness of these entities in the digestive system.

## Pharynx as a critical anatomical structure

The pharynx, also known as 'FARINGE,' is a vital anatomical structure involved in the swallowing process. It connects the mouth and nasal passages to the esophagus, facilitating the transition of food and air. Its role is crucial in both respiratory and digestive systems, making it a key focus in swallowing assessments. The pharynx is evaluated during procedures such as video fluoroscopy and VED (Videofluoroscopic Evaluation of Swallowing), underscoring its importance in diagnosing swallowing function. [Data: Entities (72), Relationships (1376, 1414)]

