## Hypercalcemia's role in cancer complications

Hypercalcemia is a common complication in patients with various cancers, particularly solid tumors. It can arise through mechanisms such as the secretion of parathyroid hormone-related peptide (PTHrP) by tumors, which increases calcium levels in the blood. This condition is particularly prevalent in hospitalized patients, indicating a need for careful monitoring and management of calcium levels in cancer patients. The intricate relationship between cancer and hypercalcemia necessitates a multidisciplinary approach to treatment, focusing on both the cancer and its metabolic consequences. [Data: Entities (5611); Relationships (6331)]

## The impact of bisphosphonates in managing hypercalcemia

Bisphosphonates are a class of medications that are crucial in managing hypercalcemia, particularly when it arises from osteoclastic activity associated with cancer. These medications inhibit bone resorption, which helps to lower elevated calcium levels in the blood. Their dual functionality in treating hypercalcemia and preventing bone loss makes them an essential part of the therapeutic arsenal for patients with certain cancers, including those at risk of osteoporosis. This highlights the importance of integrating bisphosphonates into treatment plans for cancer patients experiencing hypercalcemia. [Data: Entities (5651); Relationships (15071)]

