Staphylococcus aureus is a common bacterium that plays a significant role in causing infections, particularly in patients with medical devices such as catheters. It is recognized as a major pathogen in catheter-related infections, which can lead to severe conditions, including sepsis. The presence of Staphylococcus aureus in the bloodstream can trigger a systemic inflammatory response, further complicating the clinical picture for affected patients. [Data: Entities (4919); Relationships (10129, 13344)]

